  "notes": "The dense end of snap, and the one entry in this batch whose hats are `continuous`. The research says \"dense 16th hats\" plus \"a shaker or tambourine on offbeat 8ths\", so `fillDensity` runs 0.62–0.85 against the archetype's 0.45–0.7 and the perc block carries three lanes at `offbeat` placement — this is the busiest kit on the axis while still being a snap kit. The harmony is the other authored thing and it is unusually specific for this lane: a 4-note keyboard figure that \"implies i–VII–VI without ever stating a chord\", so `i–VII–VI` leads `progressionFamilies` at weight 7 rather than the archetype's 3, `motifPitchCount` is [4, 5] and `chords.impliedByRiff` stays true — the figure is the harmony, there is no pad. ⚠ **`confidence` is medium and the research names the inferred half**: \"lane grammar sourced, specific parameters inferred\", with the 70–80 half-time band marked [LOW-CONF], so `session.bpm` 140–160 is the weakest number here. ⛔ **The research says \"no pads or countermelody\", and this model honours that in the arrangement rather than by deleting the block.** `arrangement.sectionRules.hook` names drums, 808 and melody and stops — nothing in any authored form adds a counter — but the counter generator is still owed a part it can vary, so `countermelody.densityRatio` sits at 0.3, the floor the variety gate allows, with two styles rather than one. A model that names a counter nobody plays is more honest than one that saturates the generator. ⚠ The octave drop at the end of an 8-bar block is `bass808.slideProb` 0.1 with a single `P8` interval at `phrase_end`; the research calls it occasional and that is what 0.1 means. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts. ⚠ **The 1-bar phrase length was removed** (was [2,1], now [2,4]). A one-bar phrase looped four times carries less entropy than a four-bar phrase, and it is the countermelody it strangles hardest: a counter answers *inside* the melody’s phrase, so a one-bar shape leaves it nowhere to go. Every worst offender on `counter.rs` carried it. ⛔ The previous session recorded this exact mistake and it arrived again from the other direction — distinctness is combinatorial in the shape LENGTH, not in the option count.",
